ReadBoot Style Guide & Pattern Lab Standards
1. Documentation Philosophy
Intent: Function as a "System-First Instructional Manual" for React developers.

Tone: Neutral and competent technical instruction. No gamification or completion tracking.

Dumb Layouts: All application routes (layout.tsx, page.tsx) must be "dumb." They should specify what data to fetch (e.g., location name) and delegate the how (fetching logic, URI normalization, and error handling) to services in @repo/wp-utils.

2. Information Architecture (Mirror @repo/ui)
Documentation in apps/docs must mirror the @repo/ui filesystem hierarchy exactly:

LayoutAtoms/: Box, Prose, SectionLayout.

Molecules/: Functional groups like BouncyCTA, Masthead.

Organisms/: Complex UI sections.

Templates/: Page-level skeletons.

3. Pattern Lab Page Requirements
Every component documentation page must include these four modules:

How to Use: Pure React implementation details and prop definitions.

Interactive Preview: A live playground utilizing nuqs for linkable URL states (variants should be toggleable via URL parameters).

Accessibility Notes: Detailed documentation of ARIA roles, keyboard navigation maps, and specific WCAG targets.

Dos and Don'ts: Technical guardrails to prevent design debt and ensure consistency.

4. Technical Implementation
URI Normalization: All services must enforce trailing slashes (e.g., /about-us/) to align with the trailingSlash: true configuration in next.config.mts and prevent infinite redirect loops.

State Management: Use useQueryState from nuqs for all component modifiers in the documentation.

Styling: SCSS Modules only. Variables MUST be sourced from `packages/ui/src/tokens/**` (legacy: `patterns/Atoms/**` until removed). Never hardcode brand values. See **`readboot-scss-architecture.mdc`**.
